  "summary": "This presentation explores the concept of real-world evidence (RWE) derived from routinely collected real-world data (RWD), defining these terms through the perspectives of various regulatory and research bodies (GetReal, FDA, EMA, NICE). It categorizes diverse data sources including clinical, mobile health, medication, environmental, claims, patient-reported, molecular profiling, social media, family history, and literature. The presentation discusses the reliance on RWD in different study designs, from traditional RCTs to observational studies, and highlights regulatory experiences with RWE, particularly for post-market safety and rare diseases. It delves into types of epidemiologic research and associated caveats, introduces target trial emulation as a structured approach for RWD studies, and differentiates between efficacy and effectiveness. Key challenges in real-world observational studies—missing data, measurement error, and confounding—are addressed, alongside statistical solutions such as Quantitative Bias Analysis (QBA) and the E-Value, emphasizing the importance of data quality and explicit discussions about validity.",
  "glossary_terms": [
    {
      "term": "Real-world data (RWD)",
      "definition": "Data relating to patient health status and/or the delivery of health care routinely collected from a variety of sources.",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    },
    {
      "term": "Real-world evidence (RWE)",
      "definition": "Clinical evidence about the usage and potential benefits or risks of a medical product derived from analysis of real-world data.",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    },
    {
      "term": "Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs)",
      "definition": "Highly controlled clinical trials.",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    },
    {
      "term": "Electronic Health Records (EHR)",
      "definition": "electronic health records",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    },
    {
      "term": "Target trial emulation",
      "definition": "applying principles of randomised trials to observational studies.",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    },
    {
      "term": "Efficacy",
      "definition": "Does it work? (potentially)",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    },
    {
      "term": "Effectiveness",
      "definition": "Does it help? (in daily practice)",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    },
    {
      "term": "Quantitative bias analysis (QBA)",
      "definition": "Helps to make discussions about validity more explicit by investigating how sensitive results are to violations of certain assumptions made in the design/analysis.",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    },
    {
      "term": "Confounding",
      "definition": "a bias where a reason why patients get a certain treatment depends on the risk of developing the outcome, making a direct comparison between groups of patients who received a different treatment not valid.",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    },
    {
      "term": "E-Value",
      "definition": "Value of the joint minimum strength of confounder-exposure and confounder-outcome relation to fully explain away an observed effect.",
      "source": "llm_extracted"
    }
  ],
